  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nShortwave troughing currently over the Pacific northwest coast is\nset to move into the region today through Sunday bringing \nbeneficial precipitation to areas of southern MT and northern WY. \nSoutherly low level flow will bring increased atmospheric \nmoisture, increasing precipitable water values to 1 to 1.4 inches \nfor areas Billings east. With the lack of structure and forcing \nwith this system, storms will be slow moving (storm motion of 5-15\nmph), leading to the threat of localized heavy rain. \nAdditionally, with Hi-Res Ensembles giving far southeast MT MUCAPE\nvalues of around 500-1000 J/kg and decent effective shear values \nof around 40kts, the risk for a few severe thunderstorms is in \nplace. The best timing for these storms will be mid afternoon \nthrough around 10 pm MDT. For the remainder of southeast MT, the \nlack of shear will cause localized heavy rain to be the main \nthreat. Use caution if recreating near any burn scars over the \nweekend as conditions can quickly change with brief heavy \nrainfall. Precipitation is expected to be lighter into Sunday \nafternoon and evening as weak subsidence slightly dries the \natmosphere. Additional precipitation is expected for the same \nareas of southeast MT with a few strong thunderstorms possible \nMonday afternoon and evening. As far as amounts go, areas Rosebud\nCounty and Sheridan County, WY east have about a 50-80% chance of\ngetting at least a half inch of precipitation Saturday evening \nthrough Monday evening. Keep in mind locally higher amounts are \npossible under thunderstorms or areas of localized stronger \nforcing. By late Monday into Tuesday, higher heights move in \nbehind the low, decreasing the chance for precipitation. Ensembles\nlook to keep unstable northwest flow or brief ridging before \npotential Pacific troughing at the end of the week. Temperatures \nthrough the period will remain seasonal in the 70s to low 80s, \nincreasing into the upper 80s for the second half of next week. TS",
